developers = page('Build inside the game.', 'Lua extensions, native settings controls, and a small API you can start using today.', '''<h2>Your first native mod</h2><p>Create <code>Mods/PracticeHelper.lua</code>. Return a Lua table with a unique name, description, and optional callbacks. The runtime scans the game’s top-level <code>Mods</code> folder at startup.</p><pre><code>{
  "id": "PracticeHelper",
  "name": "Practice Helper",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Settings for my practice tools.",
  "enabled": true
}</code></pre><h2>Lifecycle and settings</h2><p>An extension returns a Lua table with optional <code>onWorldReady(api)</code>, <code>onTick(api)</code>, and <code>buildSettings(api, panel)</code> callbacks. The tick callback runs about twice a second, including while the menu is closed. Wood calls these on the game thread. Failures are logged separately so other mods can continue.</p><pre><code>return {
  buildSettings = function(api, panel)
    api.label(panel, "Practice Helper")
    api.button(panel, "Toggle helper", function()
      api.set("enabled", not api.get("enabled", true))
    end)
  end
}</code></pre><table><thead><tr><th>API</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>api.playerController</td><td>The active Unreal player controller</td></tr><tr><td>api.label(panel, text)</td><td>Add a native text label</td></tr><tr><td>api.button(panel, text, callback)</td><td>Add a native button</td></tr><tr><td>api.section(panel, title, description)</td><td>Add a themed settings section</td></tr><tr><td>api.toggle(panel, label, value, callback)</td><td>Add an On/Off control; the callback receives a boolean</td></tr><tr><td>api.slider(panel, label, min, max, step, value, callback, format)</td><td>Add a bounded slider with keyboard-friendly adjustment buttons</td></tr><tr><td>api.input(panel, hint, initial)</td><td>Add a native editable text field</td></tr><tr><td>api.refreshSettings()</td><td>Rebuild the active settings page after a reset</td></tr><tr><td>api.get(key, default)</td><td>Read a persisted setting</td></tr><tr><td>api.set(key, value)</td><td>Save a primitive setting value</td></tr><tr><td>api.log(message)</td><td>Write a runtime diagnostic</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Persistent values are stored in <code>.wood/&lt;mod-id&gt;.settings.ini</code>. Use single-line primitive values. Mod IDs may contain letters, digits, and underscores.</p><h2>Build Wood from source</h2><p>The source repository includes the installer, standalone Lua mods, UE4SS runtime, tests, documentation site, and a Windows release workflow.</p><pre><code>python -m pip install -r requirements-build.txt
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
python scripts/build_release.py
python scripts/verify_distribution.py
python scripts/package_netlify.py</code></pre><p>Build on Windows using Python 3.10. The build script verifies the pinned official UE4SS archive and produces <code>release/WoodSetup.exe</code>. No game binaries or assets belong in the source repository.</p>''')
about = page('Tools with a clear purpose.', 'Wood brings native mod management and time controls to TUNG.', '''<h2>What Wood is</h2><p>Wood is an independent TUNG modding toolkit. Its included mods cover world speed, game utilities, and Coordinates for live position readouts and teleportable waypoints.</p><h2>How it works</h2><p>A pinned UE4SS loader starts WoodRuntime inside the game. Wood creates Unreal UMG widgets for its menu and settings. The runtime scans standalone Lua files in the game’s Mods folder and stores preferences separately from game saves.</p><h2>What is included</h2><ul><li>A single-file setup executable and in-game mod menu.</li><li>Native menus and settings accessible with F10.</li><li>Wood Utilities: auto-speedrunner mode, application volume, and mouse controls.</li><li>Time Warp world-speed controls from 0.05× to 4×.</li><li>Coordinates: a live coordinate overlay and waypoint manager.</li><li>A standalone launcher and installed uninstaller.</li><li>Official mod collection and a documented settings API.</li></ul><h2>Release status</h2><p>Version 2.2.3 includes Wood Utilities and the standalone Coordinates mod. Installation and restoration are covered by regression tests. Time dilation and native widget controls were verified in the installed game during development. Compatibility with future TUNG releases is not guaranteed.</p><p>The executables are currently unsigned. This release has no automatic updater, input recording, or replay system.</p><h2>Open development</h2><p>Wood’s own source is prepared as a local Git repository with an MIT license, build instructions, and GitHub Actions release workflow. Public GitHub publication is pending. The UE4SS runtime retains its own upstream license.</p>''')
mods = page('Official mod collection.', 'A curated list of Lua mods published by the Wood curator. Mods appear here only after they are published to the online catalog.', '''<div class="catalog-toolbar"><p id="catalog-status" aria-live="polite">Loading official mods...</p><a class="textlink" href="admin.html">Curator publishing →</a></div><div id="mod-cards" class="catalog-grid" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="callout">This list comes only from the live online catalog; no sample or built-in entries are added. Downloaded Lua files run inside the game, so install only mods you trust. Copy a downloaded Lua file into the game’s top-level Mods folder and enable it with TUNG’s in-game F10 menu.</div>''')
